xteetotaller build system is generated with autoconf and automake.
If you got the sources directly from the repository, you will need to generate
the required files with `autoreconf -fi`.
If you got the sources from a distribution tarball, you can skip this step.

Requirements
============
This program is linked with the following libraries:
- libX11 (also known as Xlib)
- libXt
- Motif
Licensing information for these libraries is available in the licenses
directory.

Portability concerns
====================
I took great length to avoid the common portability pitfalls by avoiding
non-standard features.
Hence, xteetotaller should build with a toolchain conforming to the Single Unix
Specification, Version 3 (also known as POSIX.1-2001).
However, if you encounter any build issues on an UNIX-like system, please file
a bug report:
- On Github, by opening an issue

For reference, this is the setup which I used to compile xteetotaller:
- Slackware64-15.0 with and multilib testing repositories enabled
- Motif 2.3.8
- GCC 15.2.0 from the testing repository
- glibc 2.42 from the testing repository
- autoconf 2.69
- automake 1.16.2
- Mesa 25.0.7 from the testng repository
- bmake 20260912 (port of the NetBSD make tool)
- AMD Ryzen 5 2600X with MSI B450M PRO-VDH MAX motherboard
- XFX AMD RX 570 4GB

The built program was also tested on the following window managers:
- WindowMaker 0.95.9 from slackware64 repository
- emwm (Enhanced Motif Window Manager) 2.1 from slackbuilds.org

It was also tested on a FreeBSD QEMU VM with the following specifications:
- FreeBSD 15.1-RELEASE with UEFI
- qemu 10.2.4 from slackbuilds.org
- pc machine type
- virtio-vga-gl display device with virtio-gpu-qemu-kmod
- Xorg 21.1.25 with xf86-video-scfb
- emwm 2.1
- build with stock clang with modified CFLAGS and LDFLAGS to search /usr/local
- hardware acceleration and non-system installations of wine versions could not
be tested

bmake is used to check if the Makefile generated with the autoconf configure
script does not use GNU Make extensions

Basic instructions
==================
To build and install xteetotaller, simply use the following commands in the
root directory of the sources:
`./configure
make
make install`

These commands will build xteetotaller with the default build flags.
The following sections will explain how to customize build options.

configure script
================
To see the available flags for the configure script, simply type
`./configure --help`.
An useful environment variable is CFLAGS as it can be set to enable or disable
flags related to warnings or optimization.
The `--prefix` option can be set to change the installation root directory
location of the program. Its default value is `/usr/local`.

Makefile
========
The generated makefile can have a target specified for various types of build.
`make dist` will generate a tarball of the sources which can be distributed to
avoid forcing the user to generate autoconf and automake files.
`make install` will install the program to the prefix set with `--prefix`.
The `-j` flag can be used for running multiple jobs simultaneously. This is
useful for systems with multi-threading support.
